%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: factors - int & ext validity
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: What is Internal Validity in experimental research?
    Options:
    - The extent to which results can be generalized to other populations
    - The degree to which the observed effect was caused by the independent variable
      and not other factors
    - The consistency of the measurement tool
    - The truthfulness of the hypothesis
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: What is External Validity?
    Options:
    - The accuracy of the conclusions about the cause-effect relationship
    - The extent to which the results of a study can be generalized to other situations,
      populations, and times
    - The control over extraneous variables
    - The statistical significance of the findings
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a threat to INTERNAL validity?
    Options:
    - Selection bias
    - Interaction of selection and treatment
    - Multiple-treatment interference
    - All of the above
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The threat of "History" refers to:'
    Options:
    - The age of the participants
    - External events that occur during the experiment that could affect the results
    - The natural progression of time
    - A flaw in the research design
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: '"Maturation" as a threat to internal validity involves:'
    Options:
    - Changes in the measurement instrument
    - Biological or psychological changes within participants over time (e.g., growing
      tired, older, wiser)
    - Participants dropping out of the study
    - The influence of a pre-test
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "Testing" threat occurs when:'
    Options:
    - The experiment is too difficult
    - Taking a pre-test influences scores on the post-test (practice or fatigue effects)
    - The researcher tests the wrong hypothesis
    - The treatment is ineffective
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: '"Instrumentation" becomes a threat when:'
    Options:
    - A new instrument is used
    - There is a change in the calibration of the measuring instrument or in the
      observers' standards
    - The instrument is reliable
    - The same instrument is used twice
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: '"Statistical Regression" is a threat when:'
    Options:
    - Data analysis is incorrect
    - Participants are selected based on extreme scores, and their scores naturally
      move toward the average on retesting
    - The sample size is too small
    - The mean is calculated
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "Selection" threat is primarily a problem in designs:'
    Options:
    - With random assignment
    - Without random assignment, where groups are not equivalent at the start
    - With a large sample size
    - That use a pre-test
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: '"Mortality" or "Attrition" threatens validity when:'
    Options:
    - All participants finish the study
    - Participants drop out of the study differentially from the experimental and
      control groups
    - The sample is too young
    - The study is short
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a threat to EXTERNAL validity?
    Options:
    - History
    - Maturation
    - Reactive effects of testing (pre-test sensitization)
    - Instrumentation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "Interaction of Selection and Treatment" threat means:'
    Options:
    - The treatment was effective for everyone
    - The findings may only generalize to the specific type of participants selected
      for the study
    - Participants were randomly selected
    - The treatment and control groups were identical
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: '"Reactive Effects of Experimental Arrangements" refers to the
      problem that:'
    Options:
    - The lab equipment was faulty
    - The artificial setting of the experiment itself influences the results (lack
      of ecological validity)
    - The participants were reactive
    - The data was inaccurate
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: '"Multiple-Treatment Interference" is a threat when:'
    Options:
    - Only one treatment is used
    - The same participants receive multiple treatments, and the effects of one carry
      over to the next
    - The treatment is strong
    - There is a control group
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A trade-off often exists between:'
    Options:
    - Cost and time
    - Internal and External Validity
    - Theory and practice
    - The researcher and participants
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: How does "Random Assignment" primarily address threats to validity?
    Options:
    - It guarantees generalizability.
    - It controls for Selection bias by creating equivalent groups, thus protecting
      Internal Validity.
    - It eliminates the need for a control group.
    - It makes the experiment faster.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: Why is a "Control Group" crucial for controlling History and Maturation
      threats?
    Options:
    - It makes the study more expensive.
    - Any external event (history) or internal change (maturation) will affect both
      groups equally; its effect is thus canceled out when comparing them.
    - It ensures the treatment group gets attention.
    - It provides a second sample.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: How can the "Testing" threat be controlled?
    Options:
    - By using a difficult test
    - By using a "Posttest-Only Control Group Design" that eliminates the pre-test
    - By testing everyone twice
    - By using a small sample
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "Hawthorne Effect" is a specific type of threat to:'
    Options:
    - Internal Validity
    - Both Internal and External Validity
    - Only External Validity
    - Neither
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: Using a "Double-Blind Procedure" helps control which threat?
    Options:
    - Selection
    - Experimenter Bias and Participant Expectancy Effects
    - Statistical Regression
    - Mortality
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A study on a new math curriculum finds improved scores, but a
      major math competition was held during the study. This illustrates the threat
      of:'
    Options:
    - Maturation
    - History
    - Testing
    - Instrumentation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'In a long-term study on cognitive training, participants naturally
      become more practiced at the tasks. This improved performance could be mistakenly
      attributed to the training due to:'
    Options:
    - History
    - Maturation
    - Testing
    - Selection
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher uses a pre-test that is very similar to the post-test.
      Participants perform better on the post-test simply because they remember the
      questions. This is a threat of:'
    Options:
    - Instrumentation
    - Testing
    - Mortality
    - Regression
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'If a rater in a study becomes more lenient in their scoring from
      the pre-test to the post-test, the threat is:'
    Options:
    - Testing
    - Instrumentation
    - Maturation
    - History
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A school selects the lowest-performing students for a remedial
      program. Even without the program, their scores would likely improve on a retest
      due to:'
    Options:
    - Selection
    - Statistical Regression
    - Mortality
    - Diffusion
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: "Assertion (A):\_Internal validity is a prerequisite for external
      validity.\_Reason (R):\_If a study lacks internal validity (we don't know if
      the IV caused the DV), there is nothing valid to generalize."
    Options:
    - Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.
    - Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.
    - A is true but R is false.
    - A is false but R is true.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A study finds a new drug is effective for a sample of young, healthy
      males. The concern that it may not work for elderly females is a threat to
      external validity called:'
    Options:
    - Reactive Arrangements
    - Interaction of Selection and Treatment
    - Multiple-Treatment Interference
    - Testing
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: Contrast the threats of "History" and "Maturation."
    Options:
    - History is internal, Maturation is external.
    - History is an external event; Maturation is an internal process within participants.
    - They are the same.
    - History is controlled by random assignment.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A lab study on memory uses a highly artificial word-list task.
      The findings may not apply to real-world studying due to:'
    Options:
    - Testing Threat
    - Reactive Effects of Experimental Arrangements (Low Ecological Validity)
    - Selection Threat
    - Mortality
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'In a single-subject design where a patient receives Treatment
      A, then B, then A again, the effect of B might be influenced by A. This is:'
    Options:
    - Instrumentation
    - Multiple-Treatment Interference
    - Selection
    - History
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is the BEST method for increasing external
      validity?
    Options:
    - Using a very small, homogenous sample
    - Using random selection from a well-defined population and conducting the study
      in a realistic setting
    - Conducting the study in a highly controlled lab
    - Using a single group pre-test post-test design
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A flaw in a study is to have high internal validity but ignore
      external validity. This means the study:'
    Options:
    - Is worthless.
    - Can confidently claim a causal effect but we don't know if it applies to other
      people or settings.
    - Has no causal claim but is highly generalizable.
    - Is both invalid and not generalizable.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The term "Ecological Validity" is a specific type of:'
    Options:
    - Internal Validity
    - External Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Statistical Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "Placebo Effect" is a threat to internal validity that is
      best classified under:'
    Options:
    - Selection
    - Participant Bias or Expectancy Effects
    - Maturation
    - Regression
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher replicates a lab study in a school classroom to see
      if the same effect holds. The researcher is checking for:'
    Options:
    - Internal Validity
    - External Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Content Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: Using a "Solomon Four-Group Design" helps to control for which
      specific threat?
    Options:
    - Selection
    - The interactive effect of Testing and the Treatment
    - Mortality
    - History
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "John Henry Effect" (compensatory rivalry by the control group)
      is a threat to:'
    Options:
    - External Validity only
    - Internal Validity
    - Both Internal and External Validity
    - Neither
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'If the treatment group shares information with the control group,
      diluting the difference between them, it is a threat called:'
    Options:
    - Compensatory Equalization
    - Diffusion or Imitation of Treatments
    - Resentful Demoralization
    - Local History
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A "Time Series Design" with multiple pre- and post-tests helps
      to control for:'
    Options:
    - Selection
    - History and Maturation
    - Testing
    - Instrumentation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: The concept that findings from a study conducted in one culture
      may not apply to another culture relates to which threat?
    Options:
    - Temporal Validity
    - Population Validity (a type of External Validity)
    - Internal Validity
    - Measurement Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A key difference between "Subject Effects" (like the Hawthorne
      Effect) and "Experimenter Effects" is:'
    Options:
    - They are the same.
    - Subject Effects originate from participants; Experimenter Effects originate
      from the researcher.
    - Only Subject Effects threaten validity.
    - Only Experimenter Effects can be controlled.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: The threat of "Local History" is specific to which type of design?
    Options:
    - True Experiments
    - Quasi-Experiments where groups are in different locations
    - Correlational Studies
    - Pre-Experiments
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'Using "Counterbalancing" in a within-subjects design controls
      for:'
    Options:
    - Selection Bias
    - Order Effects (a type of multiple-treatment interference)
    - Statistical Regression
    - Mortality
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The most fundamental threat to internal validity that random assignment
      directly addresses is:'
    Options:
    - History
    - Selection Bias
    - Testing
    - Maturation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A study with high internal validity but conducted on a very unique
      and narrow population has low:'
    Options:
    - Population Validity (External)
    - Internal Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Statistical Conclusion Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "Reactivity" threat is most associated with participants:'
    Options:
    - Dropping out
    - Changing their behavior because they know they are being studied
    - Being randomly assigned
    - Having extreme scores
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher increases the realism of a study''s setting to improve
      its:'
    Options:
    - Internal Validity
    - Ecological Validity (External)
    - Statistical Power
    - Measurement Reliability
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The "Screw-you Effect" (Resentful Demoralization) occurs when:'
    Options:
    - The treatment group tries harder.
    - The control group performs poorly because they are demoralized about not receiving
      the treatment.
    - The experimenter makes a mistake.
    - The instruments break.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The term "Temporal Validity" refers to the generalizability of
      findings across:'
    Options:
    - Different settings
    - Different time periods
    - Different measurement tools
    - Different researchers
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
  - QuestionText: 'The single most important first step a researcher can take to
      protect the overall validity of an experiment is to:'
    Options:
    - Use a large sample.
    - Employ a strong research design (e.g., a true experiment with a control group)
      from the outset.
    - Find a significant p-value.
    - Write a good abstract.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - int & ext validity
